WEBVTT

0
00:00.400 --> 00:01.233
<v Krista>In this video,</v>

1
00:01.233 --> 00:02.550
we're going to be talking about reciprocals,

2
00:02.550 --> 00:04.800
and the only thing you need to know about reciprocals

3
00:04.800 --> 00:06.370
is that the reciprocal of something

4
00:06.370 --> 00:09.460
is whatever you can multiply this thing by

5
00:09.460 --> 00:11.870
in order to make it equal to one.

6
00:11.870 --> 00:15.040
The reciprocal is also called the multiplicative inverse,

7
00:15.040 --> 00:15.990
but of course we never use

8
00:15.990 --> 00:18.050
that term when we could just use the term reciprocal,

9
00:18.050 --> 00:19.710
because it's a lot easier to say.

10
00:19.710 --> 00:22.960
So what do we have to multiply by three over four

11
00:22.960 --> 00:24.130
in order to get one?

12
00:24.130 --> 00:25.610
Well, in fact, it's just as easy

13
00:25.610 --> 00:27.810
as flipping this fraction upside down,

14
00:27.810 --> 00:30.940
so the denominator is going to become the numerator,

15
00:30.940 --> 00:33.800
and the numerator is going to become the denominator,

16
00:33.800 --> 00:36.200
so we just flip the place of the numerator

17
00:36.200 --> 00:38.500
and the denominator, we flip this straight upside down,

18
00:38.500 --> 00:40.110
flip their places, and now

19
00:40.110 --> 00:41.800
when we multiply straight across,

20
00:41.800 --> 00:46.120
three times four gives me 12, four times three gives me 12

21
00:46.120 --> 00:48.180
and anything over itself we know is one,

22
00:48.180 --> 00:51.280
so 12 over 12 is equal to one.

23
00:51.280 --> 00:55.440
So 4/3 was what I had to multiply by 3/4

24
00:55.440 --> 00:57.480
in order to get one, and that's always going to be

25
00:57.480 --> 00:58.550
the case except when

26
00:58.550 --> 01:00.630
we have zero and we'll talk about that in a second,

27
01:00.630 --> 01:02.440
but in order to find the reciprocal

28
01:02.440 --> 01:04.350
which is whatever you have to multiply by

29
01:04.350 --> 01:07.300
in order to get one, you just flip this upside down,

30
01:07.300 --> 01:10.760
so if we have five over two, we just flip it upside down

31
01:10.760 --> 01:12.550
and we can say that the reciprocal

32
01:12.550 --> 01:16.230
is 2/5, so our reciprocal is going to be 2/5, just

33
01:16.230 --> 01:17.840
like our reciprocal was 4/3,

34
01:17.840 --> 01:19.600
and we can prove that 2/5

35
01:19.600 --> 01:22.070
is the reciprocal by multiplying straight across,

36
01:22.070 --> 01:24.410
five times two is 10, two times five

37
01:24.410 --> 01:26.830
is 10, 10 over 10 is equal to one,

38
01:26.830 --> 01:29.210
so when we multiply by 2/5, we get one,

39
01:29.210 --> 01:31.620
so we know that 2/5 is the reciprocal.

40
01:31.620 --> 01:32.540
Now, what happens when we

41
01:32.540 --> 01:34.270
try to find the reciprocal of three?

42
01:34.270 --> 01:36.140
Well, before, when we were dealing with a fraction,

43
01:36.140 --> 01:38.410
it was really easy because we just swapped the places

44
01:38.410 --> 01:40.270
of the numerator and the denominator.

45
01:40.270 --> 01:42.180
What happens when we just have a whole number?

46
01:42.180 --> 01:43.440
Well, in that case, we remember

47
01:43.440 --> 01:45.340
that this is still a fraction,

48
01:45.340 --> 01:47.560
three is actually three over one,

49
01:47.560 --> 01:48.950
and now we have a fraction,

50
01:48.950 --> 01:50.650
we flip the fraction upside down

51
01:50.650 --> 01:51.980
and we get one over three,

52
01:51.980 --> 01:53.930
so the numerator becomes the denominator,

53
01:53.930 --> 01:55.770
and the denominator becomes the numerator,

54
01:55.770 --> 01:57.950
and we say three times one is three,

55
01:57.950 --> 01:59.560
and one times three is three,

56
01:59.560 --> 02:01.130
and three over three is one,

57
02:01.130 --> 02:04.370
so we can say that 1/3 is our reciprocal.

58
02:04.370 --> 02:05.450
So when you have a whole number,

59
02:05.450 --> 02:06.990
when you just have three,

60
02:06.990 --> 02:08.860
you make sure that you say three over one,

61
02:08.860 --> 02:11.440
then you flip it upside down and you get the reciprocal.

62
02:11.440 --> 02:13.330
Now, how does that apply when we have zero?

63
02:13.330 --> 02:16.690
Well, you might think that I can say this is zero over one,

64
02:16.690 --> 02:19.160
because zero over one is still zero.

65
02:19.160 --> 02:21.850
Now I have a fraction, I just multiply by the reciprocal,

66
02:21.850 --> 02:23.550
which is what I get when I flip it upside down,

67
02:23.550 --> 02:27.090
so I do zero over one, I get zero times one is zero,

68
02:27.090 --> 02:29.640
I get one times zero is zero,

69
02:29.640 --> 02:31.970
and maybe zero over zero is one,

70
02:31.970 --> 02:34.150
because anything over itself is one.

71
02:34.150 --> 02:35.910
Well, there's so many things wrong with this it's hard

72
02:35.910 --> 02:37.130
to even know where to start.

73
02:37.130 --> 02:39.360
First of all, we can never have zero in the denominator,

74
02:39.360 --> 02:41.600
so we can't have one divided by zero,

75
02:41.600 --> 02:45.000
because that's undefined, so this doesn't work here.

76
02:45.000 --> 02:46.460
Zero over zero, first of all,

77
02:46.460 --> 02:48.240
we can't have zero in the denominator,

78
02:48.240 --> 02:51.410
and zero over zero would be zero if anything,

79
02:51.410 --> 02:54.000
but either way, this is going to be undefined also,

80
02:54.000 --> 02:56.040
so what we say is that the only number

81
02:56.040 --> 02:58.450
that doesn't have a reciprocal is zero.

82
02:58.450 --> 02:59.410
Everything else we're going to be

83
02:59.410 --> 03:00.850
able to find a reciprocal for,

84
03:00.850 --> 03:03.480
but there is no reciprocal for zero.

85
03:03.480 --> 03:04.670
What about negative numbers?

86
03:04.670 --> 03:06.800
Here we have negative 11 over four,

87
03:06.800 --> 03:09.490
so we just flip the numerator and the denominator,

88
03:09.490 --> 03:11.590
and we say four over 11.

89
03:11.590 --> 03:13.070
If I multiply straight across,

90
03:13.070 --> 03:16.140
I'm going to get 11 times four which is 44.

91
03:16.140 --> 03:19.210
I'm going to get four times 11, which is 44,

92
03:19.210 --> 03:21.350
but I have this negative sign out in front,

93
03:21.350 --> 03:23.030
so I would put a negative sign out in front

94
03:23.030 --> 03:24.440
and I would get negative one,

95
03:24.440 --> 03:26.010
but remember, the reciprocal

96
03:26.010 --> 03:28.010
is what makes this value here positive one,

97
03:28.010 --> 03:31.070
not negative one, so in order to find the reciprocal

98
03:31.070 --> 03:32.650
of a negative number, not only do

99
03:32.650 --> 03:34.800
we have to flip the numerator and the denominator,

100
03:34.800 --> 03:36.900
but we also have to include a negative.

101
03:36.900 --> 03:39.370
If I include a negative here with my reciprocal,

102
03:39.370 --> 03:41.920
then I get a negative times a negative gives me a positive

103
03:41.920 --> 03:43.530
and I end up with positive one.

104
03:43.530 --> 03:47.390
So then my reciprocal is negative four over 11, exactly what

105
03:47.390 --> 03:48.670
I get when I flip the numerator

106
03:48.670 --> 03:51.190
and denominator and keep the negative sign,

107
03:51.190 --> 03:52.260
so that's the only difference here.

108
03:52.260 --> 03:54.580
We keep the negative sign and that's important.

109
03:54.580 --> 03:55.413
What about here?

110
03:55.413 --> 03:56.520
Negative x, you can do this

111
03:56.520 --> 03:59.070
with more than just fractions or whole numbers.

112
03:59.070 --> 04:02.440
Here we have to remember that this is negative x over one,

113
04:02.440 --> 04:04.180
we're going to flip the numerator

114
04:04.180 --> 04:06.650
and the denominator and get one over x,

115
04:06.650 --> 04:08.040
but like we learned in this example,

116
04:08.040 --> 04:10.410
we need to make sure we keep the negative sign,

117
04:10.410 --> 04:12.060
then to check to make sure I did this right,

118
04:12.060 --> 04:15.220
I can say x times one gives me x.

119
04:15.220 --> 04:17.830
I can say one times x gives me x.

120
04:17.830 --> 04:20.450
I know that negative times a negative gives me a positive.

121
04:20.450 --> 04:23.900
They cancel, and I just get x over x which I know is one,

122
04:23.900 --> 04:27.823
so I can say that my reciprocal is negative one over x.